{"name":"Valour","hex":"#a3bcdb","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"valour","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Individually sustained verve or passion.","Value; worth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/valour"}],"entries":[{"title":"Courage","blocks":[{"text":"Courage is the choice and willingness to confront agony, pain, danger, uncertainty, or intimidation. Valour is courage or bravery, especially in battle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Plate_7_from_%22The_Disasters_of_War%27_%28Los_Desastres_de_la_Guerra%29-_What_courage%21_%28Que_valor%21%29_MET_MM13446.jpg/330px-Plate_7_from_%22The_Disasters_of_War%27_%28Los_Desastres_de_la_Guerra%29-_What_courage%21_%28Que_valor%21%29_MET_MM13446.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Courage","wikidata":"Q208160"},{"title":"Valour (tugboat)","blocks":[{"text":"TV Valour was an uninspected towing vessel operated by Maritrans Operation Company. Valour was built in 1975 by Main Iron Works of Houma, Louisiana and sank in the early hours of 18 January 2006 approximately 40 nautical miles (74 km) off Wilmington, North Carolina. Three crew members lost their lives."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valour_(tugboat)","wikidata":"Q7912552"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Plate_7_from_%22The_Disasters_of_War%27_%28Los_Desastres_de_la_Guerra%29-_What_courage%21_%28Que_valor%21%29_MET_MM13446.jpg/500px-Plate_7_from_%22The_Disasters_of_War%27_%28Los_Desastres_de_la_Guerra%29-_What_courage%21_%28Que_valor%21%29_MET_MM13446.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Plate_7_from_%22The_Disasters_of_War%27_%28Los_Desastres_de_la_Guerra%29-_What_courage%21_%28Que_valor%21%29_MET_MM13446.jpg/1280px-Plate_7_from_%22The_Disasters_of_War%27_%28L